<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxerxes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 424 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Artaxerxes I Longimanus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxerxes I was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a> from 465 to 424 BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>. He put down a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and his reign saw the end of open war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, traditionally through the Peace of Callias. He received the exiled Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, and in the Bible he is the king who sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nehemiah">Nehemiah</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i.jpg" alt="Artaxerxes I" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_of_Artaxerxes_I,_from_his_tomb_in_Naqsh-e_Rustam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
